<p>It collects about 150 horror and suspense films that, whether frightening, kitschy, funny, or some combination of those qualities, could well have been locally broadcast to the willing insomniacs of 50, 60, 70 years ago. <a href="https://youtu.be/WTxjEfgdvJw"><em>Revenge of the Zombies</em></a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/rXuXLoK72rw"><em>The Monster Maker</em></a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/7TVWM45tfc0"><em>Fangs of the Living Dead</em></a>, <a href="https://youtu.be/lAdeEVGXmT4"><em>The Brain That Wouldn’t Die</em></a>, <a href="https://youtu.be/lXAb2tEdJXQ"><em>Werewolf in a Girl’s Dormitory</em></a>:&nbsp;with movies like these, the entertainment — and the enticement to stay tuned, no matter the hour — began right there in the title.</p>
<p>Not that this is a playlist of guilty pleasures alone. Silent pictures like <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2024/10/watch-the-cabinet-of-dr-caligari-the-influential-german-expressionist-horror-film-1920.html"><em>The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari</em></a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2012/04/the_pixies_black_francis_creates_soundtrack_for_famous_german_expressionist_film_ithe_golemi.html"><em>The Golem</em></a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2012/10/watch_quintessential_vampire_film_nosferatu.html"><em>Nosferatu</em></a><em>, </em>and<a href="https://www.openculture.com/2013/01/watch_ihaxani_the_classic_cinematic_study_of_witchcraft_narrated_by_william_s_burroughs_1922.html"><em> Häxan </em></a>continue to be scrutinized by film-studies scholars as foundational to not just modern horror cinema but the visual language of cinema itself. However straitened their budgets, nineteen-sixties features like <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2015/10/watch-the-cult-classic-horror-film-carnival-of-souls-1962.html"><em>Carnival of Souls</em></a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2012/11/watch_inight_of_the_living_deadi_the_seminal_zombie_movie_free_online.html"><em>Night of the Living Dead</em></a>, <a href="https://youtu.be/ktvn-UFNJkM"><em>The Innocents </em></a>(<a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/10/martin-scorsese-creates-list-11-scariest-horror-films.html">a favorite of Martin Scorsese</a>), and the Edgar Allan Poe adaptation <a href="https://youtu.be/JoWNkWKrlM4"><em>The Masque of the Red Death </em></a>are now credited with merits transcending their genre. But even the less retrospectively elevated likes of <a href="https://youtu.be/12jGFmGMPh8"><em>The Murder in the Museum</em></a>, <a href="https://youtu.be/u0iCFjXvwK0"><em>The Earth Dies Screaming</em></a>, <a href="https://youtu.be/aZ2CuqGkHZ8"><em>The Little Shop of Horrors</em></a>, and&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/iOIS6fhhu6k"><em>The Psychopath </em></a>luridly demonstrate that horror could be mixed with mystery, science fiction, comedy, and psychological drama. The power of that hybridity was what late-night movie shows impressed on their most discerning viewers — which is to say, their least discerning ones.</p>
